.TH PORT_CREATE 2
.SH NAME
port_create \- create a port to receive messages (SYS_PORT_CREATE)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long port_create(const char *name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B port_create
creates a port owned by the calling process: only its threads may receive
on it with
.BR ipc_recv (2).
A server creates a named port and waits on it; clients find it by name
with
.BR port_lookup (2)
and send it messages with
.BR ipc_call (2).
.PP
.I name
is at most 15 characters; 0 or an empty string makes an anonymous port,
known only by its number (given to others in a message). Creating a named
port wakes every thread waiting for that name in
.BR port_lookup (2).
.PP
Ports are numbered in order and a number is never used again: a stale
number gives
.BR ENOENT ,
never someone else's port. A process's ports close when it ends.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The port's number (> 0), or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EEXIST
A port with this name exists already.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I name
is longer than 15 characters.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I name
is not readable.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
No kernel memory left.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR port_lookup (2),
.BR ipc_recv (2),
.BR ipc_call (2),
.BR ipc (7)
